// **The name-collision rule.** A method type parameter that reuses one of its class's names does
// NOT shadow it: the two seed one substitution, and the CLASS's binding wins — so the method's own
// parameter of that name is unreachable and its turbofish argument is checked against the class's
// type instead.
//
// Not a new rule and not one the composed spelling introduces. It falls out of the seed every
// generic call already builds: the class's arguments bind first, positionally, then everything else
// fills in with a first-wins `or_insert`. The same thing happens with no turbofish at all —
// `p.shadowed(<a B>)` on a `Pair<A, int>` is the identical `E0007`, `argument of type B is not
// assignable to A` (see `generics/class_type_param_shadowed.noe`, whose `K` reads as the method's
// only because the class's argument there happens to be the same type). Pinned on the composed
// spelling because that is the surface where both parameter lists are written out at one call site,
// and so the surface where a reader is most likely to expect shadowing.
//
// Documented rather than changed: making the inner name shadow would have to change the seed for
// EVERY generic call, inferred spellings included, and would silently retype working programs. The
// honest fix in a program is to not reuse the name — `fn shadow<U>()` says what `fn shadow<T>()`
// cannot. That is why this file's second call, on the differently-named parameter, is the one that
// runs.
// expect: error E0007 at 39:6
// expect: exit 1
struct Todo {
    id: int
}
struct User {
    id: int
}
class Repo<T> {
    // `T` again: NOT a fresh parameter here — the class's `T` wins the seed.
    fn collide<T>(x: T): string {
        return type_name::<T>();
    }

    // A distinct name is a distinct parameter, and the one that works.
    fn distinct<U>(): string {
        return type_name::<T>() ~ "/" ~ type_name::<U>();
    }
}
echo Repo::<Todo>.collide::<User>(User { id: 1 });
echo Repo::<Todo>.distinct::<User>();
